# EXPERIMENT 6

Analysis of Application Layer Packets (DNS) Using DNS Commands and Wireshark

## THEORY
The **Domain Name System (DNS)** is a hierarchical decentralized naming system for computers, services, or other resources connected to the Internet or a private network. It translates human-friendly domain names (e.g., `www.example.com`) into numerical IP addresses (e.g., `104.20.23.154`) required for identification and communication across networks.

Using command-line utilities like `nslookup` along with network packet analyzers such as **Wireshark**, administrators can query DNS servers for various records (A, MX, PTR) and inspect application-layer query-response packet exchanges.

## COMMAND-LINE EXECUTION & TERMINAL SESSION

```cmd
C:\Users\sit.lab2>nslookup [www.example.com](https://www.example.com)
Server:  SIT-CDC.SIT-CDC.SYMBINGP.SOC
Address:  10.17.28.42

Non-authoritative answer:
Name:    [www.example.com](https://www.example.com)
Addresses:  2606:4700:10::6814:179a
          2606:4700:10::ac42:93f3
          104.20.23.154
